<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nafusa Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jebel Nafusa, Jabal Nafusa, Western Mountain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nafusa Mountains are an upland range in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, long inhabited by Amazigh (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>) communities. From the 8th century the region became a stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadi Islam</a>, supporting the Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> of Tripolitania and later the Rustamid imamate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a>. It remained a base of tribal resistance, notably in the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghuma-al-mahmudi">Ghuma al-Mahmudi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> that lasted until 1858.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains.jpg" alt="Nafusa Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trtoot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Asabiya03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
